1
00:00:02,987 --> 00:00:05,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's some excerpts from Majjhima Nikaya.

2
00:00:05,930 --> 00:00:12,718
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll try and include the PDF file from which I'm reading as well, maybe in a link or so.

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this first bit is from Majjhima Nikaya 52.

4
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:22,066
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes like this.

5
00:00:23,066 --> 00:00:34,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, householder, quite secluded from central pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states, a bhikkhu enters upon and abides in the first jhāna, which is accompanied by applied and sustained thought.

6
00:00:35,709 --> 00:00:36,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's a translation.

7
00:00:37,629 --> 00:00:39,610
[SPEAKER_00]: With rapture and pleasure born of seclusion,

8
00:00:40,368 --> 00:00:42,369
[SPEAKER_00]: He considers this and understands it thus.

9
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:45,511
[SPEAKER_00]: The first jhāna is conditioned and volitionally produced.

10
00:00:46,752 --> 00:00:57,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as it says here, he considers this and understands it thus, not before or after his jhāna, but while he dwells in that jhāna with the pleasure born of seclusion.

11
00:00:58,199 --> 00:00:59,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this should dispel any notion

12
00:01:01,206 --> 00:01:06,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That some people might have that in jhāna your thinking stops and you're absorbed in some sort of experience.

13
00:01:07,328 --> 00:01:16,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a clarity of vision, unshakable mindfulness and pleasure born of seclusion from the unwholesome states, a sense of safety.

14
00:01:17,471 --> 00:01:18,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the pleasure there.

15
00:01:20,071 --> 00:01:23,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a proper thinking in it.

16
00:01:23,212 --> 00:01:26,133
[SPEAKER_00]: As in proper, it's not rooted in greed, aversion or delusion.

17
00:01:27,678 --> 00:01:31,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not affected by by central domain, by the domain of ill-will.

18
00:01:32,701 --> 00:01:36,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once the mind is established like that, he considers and understands it thus.

19
00:01:37,324 --> 00:01:42,028
[SPEAKER_00]: The first jhāna is conditioned and intentionally, volitionally produced.

20
00:01:44,869 --> 00:01:50,773
[SPEAKER_00]: But whatever is conditioned and intentionally produced is a reason on its own.

21
00:01:50,813 --> 00:01:51,994
[SPEAKER_00]: It's subject to cessation.

22
00:01:52,414 --> 00:01:53,135
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impermanent.

23
00:01:55,094 --> 00:02:13,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he recognizes that even such a lofty state, such as the first jhāna in this case, the state that it's beyond everything unwholesome, that it's utterly wholesome, as there's nothing, nothing, eh... rooted in greed, aversion, delusion in it.

24
00:02:13,404 --> 00:02:19,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And he recognizes that as pure as this state is, even this state is determined by the intention to develop this state.

25
00:02:21,715 --> 00:02:27,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's determined by his efforts, it's determined by his understanding, it's determined by the clarity of his mindfulness.

26
00:02:28,437 --> 00:02:36,059
[SPEAKER_00]: When those things cease, when his existence ceases in that manner, then this jhāna, as lofty as it is, cannot remain standing.

27
00:02:36,599 --> 00:02:47,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why the Buddha emphasizes this very often in various suttas, it's because, eh... obviously, there is no clarity of discernment when one is still rooted in unwholesome.

28
00:02:48,902 --> 00:03:04,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... when one is rooted in wholesome, such as in the case of first jhāna, if one turns his mind towards the nature of the arisen phenomena at the time, he'll see that it still shares the same nature as other things, even when the mind is not in jhāna.

29
00:03:05,547 --> 00:03:11,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Things such as cannot be owned, arisen on its own, it's impermanent, oppresses you.

30
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:16,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the characteristics of the determinations,

31
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:34,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why it's always... what we say on the basis of wholesome is just in case there are any... any... any tendencies, any other sort of subtler notions, eh... that one might not have considered, they will be considered on the basis of wholesome.

32
00:03:34,999 --> 00:03:39,900
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, eh... it will add like a... it will work like a blanket inside, so to speak.

33
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:45,702
[SPEAKER_00]: If one... if one understands that the nature of impermanence, the nature of suffering,

34
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:49,512
[SPEAKER_00]: That is subjected to its cessation on its own terms.

35
00:03:50,332 --> 00:03:59,456
[SPEAKER_00]: If one understands that on the basis of first jhāna, means nothing else inferior to that can remain unaffected by that understanding.

36
00:04:02,798 --> 00:04:04,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and that's what it says.

37
00:04:05,018 --> 00:04:06,699
[SPEAKER_00]: If we go further here, it says, eh...

38
00:04:07,499 --> 00:04:15,843
[SPEAKER_00]: If he steadies his mind in that... in that discernment, in that understanding, not in that mystical experience, but in a clarity of understanding that this is impermanent,

39
00:04:17,492 --> 00:04:19,313
[SPEAKER_00]: He attains the destruction of the taints.

40
00:04:20,634 --> 00:04:36,864
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he doesn't attain the destruction of the taints, and still has some desire for the dhamma, the delight in the dhamma, then with the destruction of the five lower fetters, he becomes one due to reappear spontaneously in the pure abode, and there attain finally bhāna without ever returning from that world.

41
00:04:37,084 --> 00:04:39,165
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he becomes, eh... anāgāmī.

42
00:04:41,897 --> 00:04:58,250
[SPEAKER_00]: This is one thing proclaimed by the Blessed One, who knows and sees accomplished and fully enlightened, wherein, if a bhikkhu abides diligent, ardent and resolute, his unliberated mind comes to be liberated, his undestroyed tends come to be destroyed, and he attains the supreme security from bondage that he had not attained before.

43
00:04:58,770 --> 00:05:05,916
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the basis of first jhāna to be used for direct insight into the nature of things.

44
00:05:09,052 --> 00:05:12,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, further, in the in the same sutta, it says, eh...

45
00:05:13,496 --> 00:05:18,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, what on earth is the path, the way to the abandoning of the five lower fetters?

46
00:05:19,061 --> 00:05:30,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, with the seclusion from the acquisitions, with the abandoning of the unwholesome states, with the calming of bodily inertia, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, it's included from unwholesome states.

47
00:05:31,209 --> 00:05:41,512
[SPEAKER_00]: A bhikkhu enters upon and abides in the first jhāna, which is accompanied by thinking and pondering, with rapture and pleasure born of seclusion.

48
00:05:42,973 --> 00:05:53,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that monk discerns whatever exists within that experience as a material form, feeling, perception, determinations and consciousness,

49
00:05:54,803 --> 00:06:05,713
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees those states as impermanent, as suffering, as disease, as a tumor, as a barb, as a calamity, as an affliction, as an alien, as disintegrating, as a void, as not-self.

50
00:06:08,492 --> 00:06:12,674
[SPEAKER_00]: While he's in that experience, that will turn his mind away from those states.

51
00:06:13,214 --> 00:06:26,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he directs it towards the deathless element, thus, this is the peaceful, this is the sublime, the stilling of all determinations, the relinquishing of all attachments, the destruction of craving, dispassion, cessation, nibbāna.

52
00:06:27,419 --> 00:06:29,760
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's steady in that, he attains the destruction of the taints.

53
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:33,802
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it goes on saying the same as for the previous passage.

54
00:06:34,703 --> 00:06:51,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's either discerning the nature of the determination, as in, even the wholesome states, they do result in a sense of safety from... and pleasure and rapture born from that seclusion, from the unwholesome states.

55
00:06:54,490 --> 00:07:03,878
[SPEAKER_00]: If one discerns the nature of them, the universal nature of the arisen phenomena, one will see that it's still subjected to same things of impermanence and suffering.

56
00:07:04,839 --> 00:07:11,104
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of that, anything inferior to it cannot remain unaffected, as we said before.

57
00:07:12,065 --> 00:07:18,010
[SPEAKER_00]: The second part, the second way, is to to look at the, eh... whatever exists in terms of the material forms of the

58
00:07:19,614 --> 00:07:42,171
[SPEAKER_00]: To look at the things, the phenomena, as in the way of aggregates, whichever... the experience as a whole, in its more general peripheral nature, which is the nature of aggregates, to see it as unwanted, to see it as dangerous, as it says here, as a disease, as a tumor, as a barb, as a calamity, as an affliction.

59
00:07:43,472 --> 00:07:47,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And he knows that by seeing it like that, again,

60
00:07:48,328 --> 00:07:56,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Any inferior desire that he might have towards particular things that are manifested through his senses, cannot remain unaffected.

61
00:07:57,250 --> 00:07:57,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

62
00:07:57,570 --> 00:08:09,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he is experiencing a thorough dispassion towards the nature of the form, the nature of the feeling, the nature of perception, determinations and consciousness.

63
00:08:10,452 --> 00:08:16,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just this particular form, or that particular feeling, or this particular perception, and so on.

64
00:08:18,032 --> 00:08:22,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the mind is properly developed in terms of that peripheral awareness.

65
00:08:22,595 --> 00:08:27,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can apply it onto that level of the nature of things.

66
00:08:27,298 --> 00:08:33,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the particular absorption with one or the other particular concern, but the fundamental nature.

67
00:08:33,762 --> 00:08:37,744
[SPEAKER_00]: If things are applied there, no particular concern can affect you.

68
00:08:40,986 --> 00:08:41,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

69
00:08:41,166 --> 00:08:43,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here's one from Majmuka 5.

70
00:08:45,713 --> 00:08:58,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, here, friend, this person with a blemish, who does not know as it really is, there is a blemish in me, he would not arouse interest and make effort to dispel that blemish.

71
00:08:59,641 --> 00:09:02,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he would die with a defiled mind, with greed, hate and delusion.

72
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:10,888
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one thing probably can be mentioned here is that it's not any random blemish.

73
00:09:11,188 --> 00:09:13,589
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like some so obvious thing that

74
00:09:15,714 --> 00:09:18,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That a person is tolerating, that he knows it's bad.

75
00:09:19,235 --> 00:09:23,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it says, he would die with defiled mind, with greed, aversion, greed, hate and delusion.

76
00:09:24,196 --> 00:09:25,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the blemishes.

77
00:09:26,696 --> 00:09:31,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that... that must be a standard and a criteria that should not be compromised.

78
00:09:32,477 --> 00:09:45,240
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, even if somebody starts with the... with the practice, eh... they can't say, okay, well, you know, I'm a putujana, so thus, of course, I have greed, aversion, delusion, but I'm still practicing.

79
00:09:48,357 --> 00:09:52,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying that, eh... removes the responsibility for greed, aversion, delusion.

80
00:09:53,839 --> 00:09:59,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And if there is a removal of responsibility for it, there cannot be effort applied for its uprooting.

81
00:09:59,220 --> 00:10:01,341
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're delaying it, you're not going to be dealing with it.

82
00:10:02,481 --> 00:10:04,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, it's not going to happen overnight.

83
00:10:05,422 --> 00:10:08,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And nobody says that you should think it will happen overnight.

84
00:10:08,943 --> 00:10:15,325
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that you should then, in the meantime, so to speak, ignore responsibility for it.

85
00:10:15,585 --> 00:10:15,725
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

86
00:10:15,985 --> 00:10:16,605
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary.

87
00:10:18,067 --> 00:10:26,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it takes you 10 years to develop the right view, well, for 10 years you have to feel the weight of responsibility for greed, aversion, delusion and never turn away.

88
00:10:26,814 --> 00:10:34,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you actually take responsibility for your own greed, aversion, delusion in such manner, it won't take you 10 years to see through it.

89
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:44,367
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why people don't see through greed, aversion, delusion is because they're delayed, because they don't take responsibility, because, as this sutta says, he does not see there is a blemish in me.

90
00:10:45,268 --> 00:10:47,229
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, what he thinks blemish is,

91
00:10:48,215 --> 00:10:52,797
[SPEAKER_00]: It's usually not... or it's like the obvious blemish, but not the blemish that the Buddha talks about.

92
00:10:53,378 --> 00:10:56,640
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, his criteria of blemish is not sufficiently developed.

93
00:10:59,841 --> 00:11:11,567
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you regard greed, aversion, delusion, doesn't matter what particular exemplification of those underlying tendencies there, all you care is that greed, aversion or delusion is there.

94
00:11:11,988 --> 00:11:12,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the blemish.

95
00:11:12,908 --> 00:11:14,489
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... that's the work there.

96
00:11:17,197 --> 00:11:18,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing less than that.

97
00:11:21,459 --> 00:11:26,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there is a second type of person, is the person with a blemish, who knows as it really is.

98
00:11:26,662 --> 00:11:27,523
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the blemish.

99
00:11:27,583 --> 00:11:28,723
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a blemish in me.

100
00:11:29,544 --> 00:11:31,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, he can then dispel that blemish.

101
00:11:35,267 --> 00:11:40,009
[SPEAKER_00]: He would die... he would die with a non-defiled mind, without greed, hate and delusion.

102
00:11:40,429 --> 00:11:40,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

103
00:11:40,589 --> 00:11:43,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he recognized that that's the threshold for... of blemish.

104
00:11:44,391 --> 00:11:45,232
[SPEAKER_00]: And he undone it.

105
00:11:46,948 --> 00:11:52,431
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose a bronze dish were bought from a shop or a smithy.

106
00:11:53,372 --> 00:12:00,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be covered with dust and stains and the owner would use it and have it cleaned and would not put it away in a dusty corner.

107
00:12:03,417 --> 00:12:06,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Would the bronze dish then get mortified and stay later on?

108
00:12:08,340 --> 00:12:08,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, friend.

109
00:12:10,677 --> 00:12:19,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, too, friend, a person with the blemish, who knows it really, there is a blemish in me, would arouse interest and make the effort to dispel that blemish.

110
00:12:20,107 --> 00:12:23,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he would die with a non-defiled mind, without greed, aversion and delusion.

111
00:12:29,176 --> 00:12:30,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, now there's again Majjhima Nikāi.

112
00:12:31,078 --> 00:12:32,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I might have read this before.

113
00:12:34,161 --> 00:12:36,243
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, it's a good passage.

114
00:12:36,263 --> 00:12:38,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Cunda, the idea that one...

115
00:12:38,585 --> 00:12:39,626
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the Buddha saying this.

116
00:12:40,226 --> 00:12:47,533
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that one who has himself sunk into the mud can pull out another who has sunk into the mud, that's impossible.

117
00:12:48,413 --> 00:12:53,418
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that the one who has not sunk into the mud can pull out another who has sunk into the mud, that's possible.

118
00:12:54,816 --> 00:13:01,219
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that the one who himself is untamed, untrained, unextinguished, can tame, train, help extinguish other, that's impossible.

119
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:06,963
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that one who himself is tamed, trained, extinguished, can tame, train, help extinguish another, that is possible.

120
00:13:11,045 --> 00:13:11,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, let's see this one.

121
00:13:11,905 --> 00:13:12,546
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bit longer.

122
00:13:13,946 --> 00:13:15,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Majjhima 13.

123
00:13:15,627 --> 00:13:16,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see what it says.

124
00:13:17,468 --> 00:13:22,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Friends, the recluse Gautama describes the full understanding of central pleasures.

125
00:13:22,851 --> 00:13:24,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And we do so too.

126
00:13:26,001 --> 00:13:32,706
[SPEAKER_00]: The recluse Gautama describes the full understanding of matter, as in rūpa, and we do so too.

127
00:13:33,266 --> 00:13:38,430
[SPEAKER_00]: The recluse Gautama describes the full understanding of feelings, and we do so too.

128
00:13:39,651 --> 00:13:41,332
[SPEAKER_00]: What then is the distinction here, friends?

129
00:13:41,613 --> 00:13:42,473
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the variance?

130
00:13:42,553 --> 00:13:50,199
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the difference between the recluse Gautama's teaching and our teaching, between his instruction and ours?

131
00:13:52,771 --> 00:13:59,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha says, because wanderers of other sects who speak thus should be questioned in this manner.

132
00:14:00,377 --> 00:14:02,618
[SPEAKER_00]: But friends, what is the gratification?

133
00:14:03,159 --> 00:14:04,119
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger?

134
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:08,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the escape in the case of sensual pleasures?

135
00:14:08,042 --> 00:14:09,223
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the gratification?

136
00:14:09,323 --> 00:14:10,184
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger?

137
00:14:10,304 --> 00:14:12,205
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the escape in the case of matter?

138
00:14:13,586 --> 00:14:14,927
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the gratification?

139
00:14:15,548 --> 00:14:16,348
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger?

140
00:14:16,529 --> 00:14:18,330
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the escape in the case of feelings?

141
00:14:20,203 --> 00:14:27,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Being questioned thus, wanderers of other sects will fail to account for the matter, and what is more, they will get into difficulties.

142
00:14:27,988 --> 00:14:28,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

143
00:14:28,609 --> 00:14:30,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not their province.

144
00:14:31,030 --> 00:14:48,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I see no one in the world with its god, its Mahārāja, its Brāhmaṇas, in its generation with the Recklessness and Brāhmaṇas, with its princes and its people, who would satisfy the mind with a reply to these questions, except for the Tathāgata or his disciple or the one who has learned it from them.

145
00:14:57,540 --> 00:15:00,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people might wonder, so why, why is that?

146
00:15:00,805 --> 00:15:06,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is, why no other sects can, other teachings can explain?

147
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:15,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, when the Buddha says, eh... the gratification, the danger and the escape, in case of sensuality, in case of matter, in case of feelings, means full understanding.

148
00:15:16,182 --> 00:15:20,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Means full understanding of the duration, of the arisen phenomena, of the sensual domain.

149
00:15:21,164 --> 00:15:25,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the particular sensuality, a particular sensual desire, but the sensual domain.

150
00:15:25,805 --> 00:15:28,366
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, possibilities of sensuality are already with it.

151
00:15:29,026 --> 00:15:30,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the domain of matter.

152
00:15:30,666 --> 00:15:31,947
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of matter.

153
00:15:32,427 --> 00:15:33,267
[SPEAKER_00]: What we said before.

154
00:15:33,387 --> 00:15:35,868
[SPEAKER_00]: The general nature of feeling, the general nature of matter.

155
00:15:36,907 --> 00:15:38,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the gratification?

156
00:15:38,790 --> 00:15:39,591
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger?

157
00:15:39,772 --> 00:15:41,134
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the escape?

158
00:15:44,158 --> 00:15:44,639
[SPEAKER_00]: All that...

159
00:15:45,702 --> 00:15:49,185
[SPEAKER_00]: is on the basis of the presently... Let's take feeling, for example.

160
00:15:49,665 --> 00:15:50,566
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the gratification?

161
00:15:50,606 --> 00:15:51,306
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger?

162
00:15:51,346 --> 00:15:52,887
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the escape in case of feelings?

163
00:15:53,368 --> 00:15:59,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these three aspects are all on the basis of the enduring feeling.

164
00:15:59,092 --> 00:16:00,533
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not three separate things.

165
00:16:00,633 --> 00:16:09,820
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just three things to discern within the presently enduring feeling, or the presently enduring matter, or the presently enduring sensual pleasures.

166
00:16:11,568 --> 00:16:22,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the gratification of these things, like the gratification of feelings, it's the... say, let's put it like this.

167
00:16:23,871 --> 00:16:30,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Gratification in the feelings is the possibility and the actuality of the pleasure you can derive from it.

168
00:16:31,807 --> 00:16:38,817
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, when it goes your way, when it obeys you, when it doesn't oppress you, when it offers even more pleasure.

169
00:16:39,397 --> 00:16:41,981
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the genuine gratification in feelings.

170
00:16:43,163 --> 00:16:47,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a genuine gratification in matter as well, for that matter.

171
00:16:47,669 --> 00:16:48,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, eh...

172
00:16:49,905 --> 00:17:03,714
[SPEAKER_00]: When the matter obeys you, when obeys your sense of control, assumed sense of control, when obeys your will, intentionality, when fulfills your projected notions of ideas and desires, there is the gratification in it.

173
00:17:03,834 --> 00:17:09,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like an offer, offer of ownership that you get verified to an extent.

174
00:17:10,418 --> 00:17:12,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, you feel gratified to that extent.

175
00:17:14,151 --> 00:17:26,281
[SPEAKER_00]: But even the offer of ownership is on the basis of that presently enduring thing, which already means it's impermanent, because you are not responsible for its presence, nor for its endurance.

176
00:17:28,223 --> 00:17:30,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these random feelings that arise,

177
00:17:32,513 --> 00:17:37,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, when you think, oh, it's given to me to be perceived, you're perceiving that as well.

178
00:17:37,675 --> 00:17:41,256
[SPEAKER_00]: That notion is also within the notion of your perception.

179
00:17:41,276 --> 00:17:41,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means,

180
00:17:58,299 --> 00:18:01,140
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not that from which the matter has been given to you.

181
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:10,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any notion that matter has been given to you is thus a wrong notion, because it requires you to step outside of yourself and to see where the matter comes from to you.

182
00:18:11,004 --> 00:18:17,087
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's inconceivable, because even that thought is within that perception that was given to you.

183
00:18:17,667 --> 00:18:19,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that matter that was given to you.

184
00:18:19,967 --> 00:18:26,589
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you recognize that there is no external that you can think of without misconceiving it.

185
00:18:26,969 --> 00:18:29,970
[SPEAKER_00]: You're fully enclosed within the five aggregates as a whole.

186
00:18:31,050 --> 00:18:34,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, experience of external, it's internal, so to speak.

187
00:18:36,071 --> 00:18:46,493
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said, eh... if a monk remembers his previous life, or five previous lives, or five hundred previous lives, all he remembers is the five aggregates or one among them.

188
00:18:47,652 --> 00:18:49,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's... that's experience as a whole.

189
00:18:49,193 --> 00:18:50,094
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing outside of it.

190
00:18:50,474 --> 00:18:52,716
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think outside of it, that's within the five aggregates.

191
00:18:53,737 --> 00:19:05,506
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not incidental that the ātavāda, the self-view, corresponds to the gratuitous notion of a self-master, controller, independent of your experience as a whole, as in external to it.

192
00:19:05,606 --> 00:19:06,786
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly where it's rooted.

193
00:19:08,188 --> 00:19:16,494
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, let's get... the gratification means that the degree that you can exercise your will, your control, your ownership,

194
00:19:17,507 --> 00:19:29,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that that... even that degree as a possibility is secondary to that independent arising of that thing, whatever that thing is, be it matter or feeling or sensuality.

195
00:19:29,830 --> 00:19:31,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see it, you take it for granted.

196
00:19:32,491 --> 00:19:40,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And taking for granted that kind of relative... offer of relative ownership means absolute ownership for you, because that's all you took, that's all you see.

197
00:19:41,915 --> 00:19:43,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the gratification.

198
00:19:43,216 --> 00:19:44,977
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the gratification in the five aggregates.

199
00:19:45,077 --> 00:19:47,258
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people remain bound with their ignorance.

200
00:19:47,899 --> 00:19:51,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because there is a mystical secret hidden behind a veil of illusion or something.

201
00:19:52,021 --> 00:19:52,161
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

202
00:19:52,541 --> 00:19:58,365
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of their own very ignorant intentions and acts that they can't say no to, pretty much.

203
00:20:00,266 --> 00:20:01,567
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it says, what is the danger?

204
00:20:03,647 --> 00:20:09,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can you can pause the talk now and think about yourself based on what we said about the gratification.

205
00:20:10,510 --> 00:20:11,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about what is the danger then.

206
00:20:12,590 --> 00:20:22,774
[SPEAKER_00]: If the gratification is the offer of that relative ownership that ends up pretty much intoxicating people, what is the danger?

207
00:20:22,935 --> 00:20:24,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Accept that intoxication.

208
00:20:24,275 --> 00:20:26,116
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger that's on the same level

209
00:20:27,014 --> 00:20:36,203
[SPEAKER_00]: As that offer prospect of pleasure, of gratification, of control, of mastery, of verification of your own assumed sense of self.

210
00:20:37,143 --> 00:20:41,608
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the danger that's simultaneously there as a second aspect?

211
00:20:44,831 --> 00:20:49,495
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is exactly rooted in the same thing where the gratification is rooted.

212
00:20:53,083 --> 00:21:04,567
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if you're just looking along the barrel of gratification, all you're gonna see is the prospects of your gratification.

213
00:21:05,228 --> 00:21:10,810
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing the nature of the basis upon which that gratification has arisen.

214
00:21:10,830 --> 00:21:13,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing the nature of the feeling, in the case we are discussing.

215
00:21:14,551 --> 00:21:32,469
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you if you don't take gratification for granted, you just see it as a prospect, as a possibility, but you don't see it as your right, and you're certainly not careless in regard to it, then you see that gratification that has arisen on the on the basis of the presently enduring feeling on its own, that I'm subjected to,

216
00:21:33,486 --> 00:21:34,507
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's dangerous.

217
00:21:35,128 --> 00:21:40,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I go with gratification, I cannot remain seeing the present enduring feeling on its own.

218
00:21:40,893 --> 00:21:41,394
[SPEAKER_00]: I will have...

219
00:21:41,674 --> 00:21:43,015
[SPEAKER_00]: I will automatically...

220
00:21:43,055 --> 00:21:45,238
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even a matter of choice, as in, I will have to do this.

221
00:21:45,598 --> 00:21:52,805
[SPEAKER_00]: You automatically, by following gratification at its face value, you automatically appropriate that which is independent of you.

222
00:21:54,126 --> 00:21:55,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Which doesn't care about you.

223
00:21:56,361 --> 00:21:58,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Your feelings don't care about you.

224
00:21:58,723 --> 00:22:00,524
[SPEAKER_00]: You are invisible to your own feelings.

225
00:22:00,764 --> 00:22:02,305
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for them, pretty much.

226
00:22:02,925 --> 00:22:04,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for the five aggregates.

227
00:22:05,007 --> 00:22:08,910
[SPEAKER_00]: You exist only because you keep assuming your own existence as the master controller of them.

228
00:22:11,812 --> 00:22:22,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't follow gratification at its face value, then you don't automatically assume yourself to be the owner of them.

229
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:26,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that.

230
00:22:26,501 --> 00:22:29,242
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't assume, you recognize how dangerous it is then.

231
00:22:30,402 --> 00:22:31,523
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the danger there.

232
00:22:32,403 --> 00:22:41,407
[SPEAKER_00]: In that there is a feeling, that offer of gratification, offer of confirmation of your ownership, offer of confirmation of your will, intentionality, whatever.

233
00:22:42,606 --> 00:22:43,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the danger.

234
00:22:43,407 --> 00:22:43,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

235
00:22:43,647 --> 00:22:49,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the offer is rooted in the presently enduring thing that you have no say in, which means it's impermanent.

236
00:22:50,132 --> 00:22:54,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It will come to cease and you can't even, like, conceive it how that would not be the case.

237
00:22:55,796 --> 00:22:56,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the danger.

238
00:22:56,636 --> 00:23:00,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking such basis for granted is then dangerous because it's going to affect you.

239
00:23:00,559 --> 00:23:06,303
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot remain indifferent if you have taken the gratification of your feeling for granted and then followed it.

240
00:23:07,104 --> 00:23:09,306
[SPEAKER_00]: When that feeling changes, you will be affected.

241
00:23:10,246 --> 00:23:11,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the danger.

242
00:23:14,287 --> 00:23:19,312
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the third aspect is understanding the escape.

243
00:23:19,832 --> 00:23:21,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the escape in the case of feelings?

244
00:23:22,294 --> 00:23:24,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this is the escape, what we just described.

245
00:23:25,097 --> 00:23:30,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Full understanding of gratification and full understanding of the danger means developing the escape.

246
00:23:32,744 --> 00:23:32,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

247
00:23:32,964 --> 00:23:33,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you

248
00:23:34,483 --> 00:23:40,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Genuinely recognize the danger of taking the arisen feeling as mine, taking it for granted.

249
00:23:40,990 --> 00:23:42,252
[SPEAKER_00]: You will turn away from it.

250
00:23:42,752 --> 00:23:48,819
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not entertain that notion of appropriation and gratification for not even for a second longer.

251
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:51,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the most perilous thing.

252
00:23:52,283 --> 00:23:53,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, you escape it.

253
00:24:00,622 --> 00:24:04,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's in Majjhima Nikaya 13, for those who want to read it.

254
00:24:04,725 --> 00:24:07,166
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, we expanded on it a bit more.

255
00:24:11,670 --> 00:24:15,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, here's Majjhima 15, that's removing the blemishes, we talked about it.

256
00:24:18,794 --> 00:24:23,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, here is an interesting passage, I think.

257
00:24:25,809 --> 00:24:43,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's Majjhima 17, and it says here, because a bhikkhu abides supported by a person, when abiding, when abiding supported by a person, by a certain person, I suppose, unestablished mindfulness does not get established.

258
00:24:45,607 --> 00:24:49,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Unconcentrated mind, uncomposed mind does not get composed.

259
00:24:52,158 --> 00:24:54,219
[SPEAKER_00]: That bhikkhu should thus reflect.

260
00:24:54,239 --> 00:24:56,479
[SPEAKER_00]: I abide supported by this person.

261
00:25:21,486 --> 00:25:31,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And abiding thus, my unestablished mindfulness does not get established, uncomposed mind does not get composed, undestroyed desire does not get destroyed, unattained noble end of yoke is not attained.

262
00:25:32,508 --> 00:25:40,790
[SPEAKER_00]: As for the four requisites, robes, food dwellings, and medicine, I collect them without difficulty.

263
00:25:43,810 --> 00:25:45,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That bhikkhu should then reflect further.

264
00:25:45,631 --> 00:25:46,711
[SPEAKER_00]: I did not go forth.

265
00:25:47,966 --> 00:25:53,329
[SPEAKER_00]: As a homeless, for the sake of robes, food, dwellings or requisites, when I'm ill.

266
00:25:54,449 --> 00:26:02,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, to me, living supported by this person, not established mindfulness does not get established, not concentrated mind does not concentrate, not destroyed desire does not get destroyed.

267
00:26:04,214 --> 00:26:11,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And not attained noble end of yogic is not attained, because that bhikkhu should leave that person without informing them, so that he may not follow.

268
00:26:12,478 --> 00:26:14,619
[SPEAKER_00]: This, eh... this refers to the...

269
00:26:14,939 --> 00:26:16,320
[SPEAKER_00]: I think this refers to the teacher.

270
00:26:17,937 --> 00:26:28,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this refers to a bhikkhu who would live with, under the mentorship, I suppose, of a senior monk, but he's not progressing.

271
00:26:29,347 --> 00:26:36,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the monastery might be well supplied by everything that he might need in terms of the requisites, but then he should reflect that that's not why he went forth.

272
00:26:37,515 --> 00:26:43,681
[SPEAKER_00]: And he should thus leave, not just leave right away, but leave without telling that person so that they can't follow him.

273
00:26:46,620 --> 00:26:51,602
[SPEAKER_00]: It means really prioritizing and reminding yourself why you got into robes in the first place.

274
00:26:53,183 --> 00:26:54,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the life won't last forever.

275
00:26:56,445 --> 00:26:59,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if a person lives 50 years more, that's nothing.

276
00:26:59,827 --> 00:27:00,947
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to go very quickly.

277
00:27:02,248 --> 00:27:09,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And without thinking about the end game of, as the Buddha said himself, not attain the noble end,

278
00:27:12,693 --> 00:27:40,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, one is going to pay the price he probably wasn't ready to bargain for, because the fact that the person was able, first, to come across the Buddha's teaching, secondly, able to leave the household life and actually ordain, that's already quite a fortunate set of circumstances, in a way.

279
00:27:41,522 --> 00:27:44,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, nobody's denying responsibility of people who do that.

280
00:27:44,485 --> 00:27:59,637
[SPEAKER_00]: They are responsible for their own going forth and everything, so they will take the credit for it, but all of that, in itself, will be a wasted opportunity if a person does not attain the noble end.

281
00:28:00,493 --> 00:28:12,559
[SPEAKER_00]: If they get satisfied with just being a monk or living in a suitable environment, they need to then reflect further and genuinely see whether their mindfulness gets increased, their composure, the right view gets developed.

282
00:28:13,159 --> 00:28:15,721
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, well, there is no time to waste.

283
00:28:16,101 --> 00:28:18,482
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much what this passage is saying.

284
00:28:28,628 --> 00:28:30,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see where this one takes us.

285
00:28:30,649 --> 00:28:40,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Majjhima 11 and says, because there are these two views, the view of being and the view of non-being.

286
00:28:41,995 --> 00:28:44,236
[SPEAKER_00]: The word for being is bhāva, the Pali word.

287
00:28:46,417 --> 00:28:56,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ascetics and brahmins who adhere to the view of being, who are overpowered by the view of being, who hang on to the view of being, they are opposed to the view of non-being.

288
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:08,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ascetics and brahmins who adhere to the view of non-being, who are overpowered by the view of non-being, who hang on to the view of non-being, they're opposed by the... they're opposed to the view of being.

289
00:29:10,288 --> 00:29:20,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ascetic and brahmin who do not understand as they actually are the origin, setting down, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of these two views.

290
00:29:21,134 --> 00:29:23,576
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what we talked about just now.

291
00:29:25,057 --> 00:29:27,759
[SPEAKER_00]: If they don't understand those views fully,

292
00:29:28,588 --> 00:29:30,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ascetic in Brahman who does understand

293
00:29:55,585 --> 00:29:57,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's not much to add here, unless...

294
00:30:24,762 --> 00:30:30,526
[SPEAKER_00]: We begin a whole new long discussion about what being is and what non-being is.

295
00:30:31,087 --> 00:30:39,033
[SPEAKER_00]: But practically, even passages like this, a person should at least try to understand.

296
00:30:39,053 --> 00:30:43,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Not dismiss, oh, it's just some like generic philosophy or something, because it isn't.

297
00:30:44,257 --> 00:30:50,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Being and non-being, again, in the most fundamental, the most general, peripheral sense,

298
00:30:54,235 --> 00:30:57,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty much either-or.

299
00:30:58,037 --> 00:31:00,019
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, it's a... it's a...

300
00:31:00,879 --> 00:31:04,742
[SPEAKER_00]: If you push things peripherally, you're gonna say they're there or they're not there.

301
00:31:05,582 --> 00:31:07,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, the being is there.

302
00:31:08,044 --> 00:31:11,326
[SPEAKER_00]: The denying of the being, then you have the non-being.

303
00:31:12,587 --> 00:31:19,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that non-being, you can designate, you can have a view rooted in it, means even that is a thing.

304
00:31:20,011 --> 00:31:22,693
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just the negative denial of the being.

305
00:31:24,340 --> 00:31:35,086
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Buddha talks like this about the bhava and vibhava, it refers to the absolutely fundamental, most general underlying view of a person.

306
00:31:36,406 --> 00:31:50,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And if a person himself pushes and tries to discern his own views as far as he can, he'll see that he's going to be rooted in either fundamentally saying yes to his existence or saying no to his existence.

307
00:31:53,345 --> 00:31:56,487
[SPEAKER_00]: On more particular levels, he might sometimes say yes, sometimes say no.

308
00:31:56,827 --> 00:32:02,330
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, he's going to have a view of yes towards it, or no towards it.

309
00:32:02,370 --> 00:32:03,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what this means.

310
00:32:03,671 --> 00:32:09,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the annihilationists and internalists, as we will call them.

311
00:32:09,035 --> 00:32:09,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it wasn't just like...

312
00:32:13,295 --> 00:32:16,378
[SPEAKER_00]: A slur that he developed to dismiss other philosophers of notes.

313
00:32:16,438 --> 00:32:23,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you press those wrong views, they're going to fall into these two categories on the most fundamental, general level.

314
00:32:24,746 --> 00:32:27,830
[SPEAKER_00]: The ones that say yes, and the ones that say no.

315
00:32:29,311 --> 00:32:34,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, for as long as that's the case, then there is lust, anger, delusion, and assumption out there.

316
00:32:37,220 --> 00:32:40,641
[SPEAKER_00]: If one understands that, that's how one develops the Middle Way.

317
00:32:41,121 --> 00:32:44,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how one is free from lust, anger, delusion and assumption.

318
00:32:44,741 --> 00:32:45,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Free from ignorance.

319
00:32:53,323 --> 00:33:03,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on a practical basis, a person can reflect, just through asking certain questions to oneself, pretty much.

320
00:33:05,978 --> 00:33:19,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing what the notion is... what kind of attitude is present there, for example, eh... when you think about death, the inevitable death, that will have to happen, that nobody wants to think about.

321
00:33:21,364 --> 00:33:23,645
[SPEAKER_00]: To what degree is my sense of self affected?

322
00:33:26,345 --> 00:33:30,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, most people, if you just pick someone on the street, they say, well, everybody dies, that's it.

323
00:33:30,847 --> 00:33:32,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize they don't really allow

324
00:33:33,377 --> 00:33:48,024
[SPEAKER_00]: the notion of death to affect their sense of self, because it's way too terrifying, which means they fully... they fully kind of encapsulated themselves in a... in a... in a... this protective view of... of my being will be unaffected.

325
00:33:49,624 --> 00:33:57,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, they... on the fundamental level, they refuse to say... they refuse to even allow possibility of no towards their own being.

326
00:33:59,529 --> 00:34:00,249
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always a yes.

327
00:34:01,269 --> 00:34:02,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And even when they face death,

328
00:34:04,838 --> 00:34:07,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally witness it.

329
00:34:08,381 --> 00:34:10,543
[SPEAKER_00]: They make sure it doesn't apply to them through their view.

330
00:34:10,563 --> 00:34:14,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Their views protect them from having it applied to themselves.

331
00:34:15,807 --> 00:34:16,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Until, of course, it's too late.

332
00:34:17,068 --> 00:34:19,830
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't hide from it forever.

333
00:34:20,711 --> 00:34:21,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Death will come.

334
00:34:21,712 --> 00:34:29,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it hasn't been faced while you were alive, then it's too late to deal with it.

335
00:34:29,618 --> 00:34:30,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-being would be the opposite.

336
00:34:30,659 --> 00:34:32,040
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be the denial of the being.

337
00:34:33,148 --> 00:34:36,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Which again, fundamentally, is a form of being in itself.

338
00:34:37,031 --> 00:34:41,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's equally binding as a wrong view.

339
00:34:41,855 --> 00:34:49,081
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just through denying it, you assume that what's left after the denial, it's permanent, it's gonna last forever.

340
00:34:49,561 --> 00:34:50,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you adhere to.

341
00:34:52,244 --> 00:35:00,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, if somebody says, well, see, through denying it, you're still within the nature of being, they will get angry and upset.

342
00:35:02,542 --> 00:35:03,843
[SPEAKER_00]: They would oppose those views.

343
00:35:04,424 --> 00:35:05,945
[SPEAKER_00]: They would oppose the views of being.

344
00:35:06,345 --> 00:35:08,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Not of a particular being, this and that.

345
00:35:08,927 --> 00:35:22,037
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fundamental, like, say, like, for example, saying, quite a common view, that we are nothing, but just a bunch of molecules, hormones and organs operating, and just, just basically a physical matter.

346
00:35:22,077 --> 00:35:23,678
[SPEAKER_00]: When the body breaks apart, that's it.

347
00:35:23,778 --> 00:35:24,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nothing.

348
00:35:25,099 --> 00:35:25,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing left.

349
00:35:25,900 --> 00:35:26,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing moves.

350
00:35:26,540 --> 00:35:27,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing comes.

351
00:35:27,261 --> 00:35:27,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

352
00:35:27,961 --> 00:35:28,762
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the end of it.

353
00:35:29,513 --> 00:35:33,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a form of just denying the validity of the being of your experience as a whole.

354
00:35:34,596 --> 00:35:44,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Just dismissing it through scientific terms and scientific observations and attributing your own responsibility and properties just to the molecules and hormones and chemicals and whatnot.

355
00:35:45,921 --> 00:35:54,406
[SPEAKER_00]: But if somebody says, well, see, even that denial that you are engaging in, and then you would be doing so,

356
00:35:55,242 --> 00:36:01,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because through dismissing it, you think you're safe from that which is subjected to death.

357
00:36:01,024 --> 00:36:02,265
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the implication of it.

358
00:36:02,905 --> 00:36:13,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, if you were to point out that the notion, eh... in regard to the dismissal of the being, the notion that kind of... it's... it is a background of it.

359
00:36:14,009 --> 00:36:15,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is within the being.

360
00:36:15,850 --> 00:36:19,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to deny something, that thing needs to be present there.

361
00:36:20,351 --> 00:36:21,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And they have no say in it.

362
00:36:22,636 --> 00:36:27,440
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, whether you affirm the being or deny being, that is secondary to the presently arisen bhava.

363
00:36:29,321 --> 00:36:37,347
[SPEAKER_00]: And, whether you affirm the being or deny being, you just end up binding yourself more to the assumption of the validity of that being in the first place.

364
00:36:38,088 --> 00:36:44,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you end up binding yourself even more to that which is subjected to death and destruction.

365
00:36:45,985 --> 00:36:56,394
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said when people don't know the escape from being, they end up revolving around the being, through affirming, denying, affirming and denying, neither affirming nor denying, they end up being bound with the being.

366
00:36:56,794 --> 00:36:58,716
[SPEAKER_00]: He said it's like a dog chained to a post.

367
00:36:59,637 --> 00:37:05,282
[SPEAKER_00]: It tries to escape the post, ends up just running around the post and tying himself tighter and tighter and tighter.

368
00:37:07,324 --> 00:37:10,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that.

